"settings.help.whatsNewTitle" = "What's New";
"settings.help.whatsNewBody" = "v2.23.3 — Fixed: the action bar appears again when you select text in a Tippi note (it stopped showing there in 2.23.1).\n\nv2.23.2 — Fixed: dictating into a note really no longer crashes (the 2.23.1 fix was not enough).\n\nv2.23.1 — Fixed: dictating into a note no longer crashes. The action bar only appears when you select text yourself (drag, double-click, shift-click), and a click elsewhere closes it right away.\n\nv2.23.0 — Lists in notes: three buttons at the bottom of the notes window turn the selected lines into a bulleted, checklist or numbered list. Return continues a list, Return on an empty item ends it, a click on [ ] checks it off. The bottom right now counts lines too.\n\nv2.22.1 — The recording indicator no longer has a square edge, the end of your last word stays in the recording, and dictation, the popup mic and translate no longer get in each other's way. Live text now also shows up reliably on the first dictation after launch.\n\nv2.22.0 — Dictation now shows what Tippi hears while you speak: turn on “Show text live while recording” under Dictation (Parakeet only, off by default), in three text sizes. The recording indicator can sit in six places. If you switch microphones mid-dictation, for example to AirPods, Tippi keeps recording on the new one.\n\nv2.21.0 — Dictation for emails (⌃⌥⌘B): Tippi sets the greeting, paragraphs and sign-off for you, while your normal dictation stays plain for chats. Line breaks and punctuation are also available as a switch under Dictation. Autocomplete continues your sentence instead of answering questions and no longer glues words together. The currency converter shows the result and copies it. In Notes you can hide the sidebar (⌃⌘S).\n\nv2.20.1 — The prompt popup now opens right at your selection or text cursor instead of at the mouse pointer, on a second display too. Autocomplete (Labs) only uses your custom words when they fit what you are typing. The snippet option for the week starting Thursday has a neutral name.\n\nv2.20.0 — New app-family design: the \"Slate\" color palette with a calm slate blue, the new typeface Plus Jakarta Sans, and tinted glass so the selection bar and popups no longer take on the colors of the app underneath. Prominent buttons are now easy to read in dark mode, too.\n\nv2.19.0 — Currency converter: select an amount (23 €, 62,700 円), click €→$ in the selection bar and pick a favorite currency — the conversion is appended. “Generate password” in the menu bar and popup: 12 characters, inserted at the cursor, on the clipboard for 60 seconds. New quick actions: highlight, bullet list, quotation marks, square and curly brackets. The selection bar now has two rows and shows the character count without a click.\n\nv2.18.0 — New Permissions tab: every permission with its status and a button to grant it. Your own snippets appear in the “:” list ahead of emoji. Autocomplete now works in Obsidian.\n\nv2.17.1 — The Settings window shows its content again (it was blank since 2.17.0).\n\nv2.17.0 — Edit built-in prompts and reorder all prompts by drag & drop (number keys 1–9 follow the order); edit imported snippets; choose both autocomplete keys (⇥ word, ⇧⇥ all); new suggestion popup as a glass capsule with key hint; tidier settings (own Autocomplete section, “Dictation”, provider cards collapsible).\n\nv2.16.3 — App lists in Settings show icon and name instead of the internal bundle ID; add apps via the “Add app…” menu, including apps that are not running.\n\nv2.16.2 — Audit follow-up: AI results stay with the original app; offline notes and iCloud edits are both kept as separate copies; deleted notes stay deleted; damaged history cannot crash Tippi; incomplete AI streams are rejected; Accessibility searches are time-limited; a newer clipboard copy is not overwritten.\n\nv2.16.1 — Code review, part 2: dictation cleanup now really runs at a low temperature, notes no longer lose text on ⌘Q or failed saves, a new hot key applies right away, API keys can be removed, “Calendar week” is now “Week starting Thursday”, Esc cancels the screen selection, and many small bugs are fixed.\n\nv2.16.0 — New: press Return after dictation, per app (Settings → Voice) — handy for the Claude app. In terminals only if AI cleanup left your text unchanged. When AI cleanup fails, Tippi now tells you why. Fixed: notes no longer lose edits from your other Mac, custom prompts survive syncing, no more doubled text, empty AI answers never delete your selection, no crash for screen captures across two displays, snippets no longer delete unrelated characters after a click, dictation in the Notes window lands in the note.\n\nv2.15.0 — New (Labs, off by default): autocomplete while you type. After a short pause your local model suggests the rest of the sentence in grey at the cursor — ⇥ takes it word by word, or just keep typing. Works in Mail too. Everything stays on your Mac, password fields are never read. Turn it on from the menu bar → Autocomplete. Also: your dictation mode now survives updates, and custom words are shown at a readable size.\n\nv2.14.0 — Custom words now understand pronunciation variants: “Tipi → Tippi” turns every heard “Tipi” into your “Tippi”, without AI and even with cleanup off. New under Settings → General: Appearance System · Light · Dark for the whole app. And Tippi’s Dock icon can be kept — one click opens Notes.\n\nv2.13.0 — OpenAI's default is now GPT-6 Luna: half the price of GPT-5.6 Luna, and Tippi switches its internal reasoning off so short rewrites come back without a thinking pause. Anthropic's premium pick is Claude Opus 5.5. Two OpenRouter models had wrong ids (Gemini Flash, Claude Haiku) — fixed, and stored selections are moved over automatically.\n\nv2.12.5 — When Tippi finds an update on its own, it no longer opens a window behind your other apps. Instead you get the notification “Tippi … is available”, and the menu says “Update to … ready”. A click opens the update window in front.\n\nv2.12.4 — The selection bar now closes when you move on: as soon as the pointer moves well away from the selected text and the bar, or you switch to another app — not only after five seconds. It also no longer flashes back up when you then click somewhere that leaves the selection intact (menu bar, Dock, a toolbar). Select the text again to bring it back.\n\nv2.12.3 — Screen text recognition froze the screen too late. The selection overlay takes focus, which closes any pop-up, menu or tooltip — and the capture only happened after you had dragged, so anything focus-sensitive was already gone. The screen is now frozen the instant you press the shortcut and the selection runs on that still. A missing Screen Recording permission also surfaces before you drag now, not after. True menus remain out of reach: they swallow global shortcuts.\n\nv2.12.2 — New: join wrapped lines. Recognition returns one entry per screen line, so pasted text used to break mid-sentence. Paragraphs, bullets and sentence endings are preserved, hyphenated breaks are joined. Switch it off for code or tables.\n\nv2.12.1 — Screen text recognition captured the wrong region: the selection rectangle and the capture count Y coordinates in opposite directions, so without the conversion a mirrored area was taken. A missing permission also looked like missing text, because ScreenCaptureKit returns a black image instead of an error. Both fixed, and a blank capture now explains what to do.\n\nv2.12.0 — New: pull text out of a screen selection (⌥⌘2, must be enabled under Hotkeys). Recognition is local, nothing is stored or sent, and the text deliberately does not hand off to your iPhone. See “Text from a screen selection”.\n\nv2.11.9 — This Help had fallen five versions behind and is current again. “Notes” explains cross-Mac syncing and why the note you are typing in is never overwritten. “Troubleshooting” explains the new menu-bar readout and the notification. “MLX” explains what happens when an old server process is holding the port. “Voice” says how conservative dictation cleanup is, and why.";
"settings.help.screenOCRTitle" = "Text from a screen selection";
"settings.help.screenOCRBody" = "Select a region of the screen and pull the text out of it — for anything that can't be copied: screenshots, image-only PDFs, video stills, schedules published as graphics.\n\nPress the hotkey (default ⌥⌘2), drag a rectangle, done. The dimensions appear while you drag.\n\nThe screen is **frozen** the instant you press the hotkey, and the selection happens on that still. That is why it also works on a **pop-up, dialog or tooltip** that would close the moment you click elsewhere — it is in the picture regardless of whether it is still open for real. You also see exactly what was captured. True **menus** stay out of reach: they swallow global shortcuts, so the hotkey never arrives. Cancel with Esc, right-click, or let it time out after 60 seconds.\n\nRecognition runs entirely on your Mac (Apple's Vision, German and English). Nothing is sent, nothing is stored — no history, no file. The captured image lives only in memory and is gone once the text is out.\n\nThe feature ships OFF and must be enabled under Hotkeys. The reason: it needs the Screen Recording permission, and that permission is permanent — once granted, Tippi could read the screen at any time. If you don't need the feature, you should never have to grant it. macOS asks the first time you trigger it; Tippi then needs a restart.\n\nThe recognised text is NOT handed off to iPhone or iPad — the clipboard is deliberately limited to this Mac. A selection may contain a password, and that should not leave the device.\n\nLine breaks: recognition returns one entry per screen line, so pasted text would break mid-sentence. The option “Join wrapped lines” (on by default) reassembles paragraphs while keeping what carries meaning: blank lines, sentence endings, colons, bullets and numbered lists. A hyphen at the end of a line is joined without a space. Switch it off for code or tables.\n\nIf you use a clipboard manager (Raycast, Alfred, Paste), the text lands there like any copied text. For confidential captures, enable “Hide from clipboard history” under Hotkeys — those apps will then skip the entry.";
"settings.help.howTitle" = "How Tippi works";
"settings.help.howBody" = "Select text in any app, then press your hotkey (default ⌥⌘T). A popup appears near your cursor — pick a prompt, preview the result, and replace or copy.";
"settings.help.instructTitle" = "Give an instruction — type or speak";
"settings.help.instructBody" = "Besides the built-in prompts, you can give Tippi a free-form instruction for the selected text. Trigger Tippi with text selected — an input field sits at the bottom of the popup. Type your instruction (e.g. \"reply to this email politely\", \"translate to Spanish\") and press Return, or tap the mic button and speak it. Tippi follows the instruction literally: transform instructions (translate, summarize, shorten) operate on the text as-is, while reaction instructions (reply, respond) produce an answer. The field auto-focuses so you can type right away; press ↓ to jump back to the prompt list.";
"settings.help.previewTitle" = "Live preview & refine";
"settings.help.previewBody" = "The result streams in token by token, so you see it forming instead of waiting for the whole answer. Once it's ready you can refine it in place: type a follow-up in the \"Refine\" field (e.g. \"shorter\", \"more formal\", \"add a greeting\") and it rewrites the current result — chain as many refinements as you like. Keyboard shortcuts in the preview: Return = Replace, ⌘C = Copy, ⌘Return = Append, ⌘R = Regenerate, Esc = Cancel. If a result hits the model's length limit it's kept but flagged \"Cut off\" so you don't replace a truncated text by accident.\n\nAn interrupted OpenAI-compatible stream is an error, not a finished answer: its partial text cannot replace your selection.";
"settings.help.promptsTitle" = "Create, edit & reorder prompts";
"settings.help.promptsBody" = "The Prompts tab shows every prompt in one list — built-in and custom mixed, in popup order. Drag the handle to reorder; the first nine get the digit keys 1–9, custom prompts included.\n\nNew prompt: a title, an SF Symbol name and an instruction for the AI. The selected text is sent automatically — no placeholder needed.\n\nEdit (pencil) works for every prompt, built-in ones too: title, symbol and instructions, and the steps of the built-in chain. Only what you change is stored — an unchanged title stays in your language. Edited built-ins are marked and can be reset to the original at any time. The edit window is also where you pick which AI provider runs the prompt.\n\nEdits and order travel to your other Macs via iCloud. New prompts are added at the end of the list.";
"settings.help.chainsTitle" = "Prompt chains";
"settings.help.chainsBody" = "In the prompt editor switch the type to Chain to run several prompts in a row. Each step's output feeds the next — e.g. Cleanup → English translates your text after fixing grammar. The preview shows the current step; if a step fails, the last good result stays editable. Try the built-in \"Cleanup → English\".";
"settings.help.localActionsTitle" = "Local quick actions";
"settings.help.localActionsBody" = "Tippi is not PopClip: marking text alone does not open the menu. Select text, then press your hotkey (default ⌥⌘T) or choose Trigger Tippi… from the menu bar.\n\nWhen text is captured, Quick actions appear above the AI prompts. They run locally: rich-text formatting, bullet list, quotation marks, parentheses/square/curly brackets, case changes, underscore/hyphen, join lines, and counts.\n\nBold/Italic/Underline/Strike use RTF when the app supports it; plain-text apps get Markdown-style fallbacks (**bold**, ~~struck~~). Highlight (yellow background) only works in apps with formatting — plain-text apps keep the text unchanged. Bullet list puts “- ” before every line. Counts only show a number and do not edit text.\n\nConvert currency: when the selection is an amount (23 €, 62,700 円, USD 1,100), €→$ appears — pick a favorite currency. Favorites in Settings → General. By default your text stays unchanged: a small hint at the selection shows “≈ 26.19 $ · copied”, and the converted amount is on the clipboard. Under “Result” it can also be appended to the text: 23 € (≈ 26.19 $), hint “≈ 26.19 $ inserted · copied”. Rates By Exchange Rate API, fetched once a day; your selected text is never sent.\n\nGenerate password (also in the menu bar): 12 characters with upper/lower case, digits and symbols, inserted at the cursor. It then stays on the clipboard for 60 seconds (for “repeat password” fields) — this Mac only, no Handoff, hidden from clipboard managers — before your previous clipboard comes back.\n\nIf you see “No text captured”, grant Accessibility for the Tippi.app you launched (local test builds need a fresh toggle after each make build). TextEdit and Notes work best. Hide Quick actions in Settings → General.";
"settings.help.troubleBody" = "Nothing happens when you only highlight text? Press ⌥⌘T (or ⌃⌥⌘T) after selecting — Tippi does not auto-open like PopClip.\n\nWhich permissions are missing? Settings → Permissions shows all of them at a glance, with a button to grant each.\n\nHotkey not firing? Grant Accessibility (System Settings → Privacy & Security, then enable Tippi in the accessibility list (renamed on macOS 26+)). Trigger via menu bar ✏️ → Trigger Tippi…\n\nQuick actions show but text does not change? Re-select text in TextEdit/Notes, trigger again, then tap the action. Local builds: enable Accessibility for build/Build/Products/Release/Tippi.app and restart the app.\n\nNew hotkey not responding? Quit and reopen Tippi.\n\nWhat is broken? The menu bar says so now: when a red triangle appears, the line next to it names the cause (e.g. “Local server is not running”), and the next step sits directly below it. Clicking that row opens the place in Settings where it gets fixed. Tippi also announces a failure on its own — once per new problem, silently. If you do not allow notifications, the menu still carries everything.\n\nCan't see an update window? When Tippi finds an update on its own, it deliberately opens no window — macOS would put it behind your other apps. Instead you get the notification “Tippi … is available”, and the menu says “Update to … ready”. Clicking either opens the update window in front.";
"settings.help.variablesTitle" = "Prompt variables";
"settings.help.variablesBody" = "Use {placeholders} in your prompt instructions — Tippi fills them in at trigger time:\n\n{clipboard} — your current clipboard content\n{app_name} — the app you're writing in (e.g. Mail, Safari, Slack)\n{language} — detected language of your selected text (e.g. German)\n{selected_text} — the selected text itself\n\nExample — app-aware tone:\n\"Rewrite for {app_name}. In Slack: casual, max 2 sentences. In Mail: formal.\"\n\nExample — clipboard as style reference (copy a sample text first):\n\"Match the tone of this reference: {clipboard}\"\n\nExample — always stay in the right language:\n\"Improve the text. Stay in {language}. Return only the result.\"\n\n⚠️ Privacy note on {clipboard}: whatever is in your clipboard becomes part of the prompt and is therefore sent to your AI provider. Don't use this placeholder while a password, API key or other sensitive data is copied — or pick a local provider (MLX/Ollama) for such prompts, where nothing leaves your Mac.";
